export const essays = {
  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-health-issues.html": `<p>Silk on a tiny frame still hides a mouth that goes sour early. I have held a Yorkshire Terrier on a towel while the doctor lifted the lip, and the smell arrived before the probe did. Dental disease is the everyday failure in this toy terrier. Tartar climbs the canines, gums redden along the gumline, and a tooth loosens while the dog keeps eating. I book a dental chart by age 3 so a small mouth is cleaned before the roots are the only thing left holding a molar.</p>
<p>The kneecap is the limp people excuse. Luxating patellas show up as a skip, a hop, then a trot that looks pleased with itself. Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is harsher in a young adult. The thigh muscle thins, the hip hurts, and the puppy who used to bounce sits out the jump onto the couch. Patella evaluation on the parents is the paperwork I read first. I like those records filed where the <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC program for patella records</a> keeps orthopedic papers together. I palpate the stifle again when the dog turns 5, because a mild slip at two can be a loud slip later.</p>
<p>Collapsing trachea sounds like a goose honk when excitement spikes or a collar yanks the neck. Those dogs wear a harness in my house, and the room stays cool when they pant. A portosystemic shunt is quieter. The puppy stays minute, acts vacant after eating, and may press the head into a corner or stagger on the kitchen tile. That pattern needs bloodwork and imaging. A bigger dinner leaves the shunt where it was. Progressive retinal atrophy takes night vision first. Furniture that was easy at dusk becomes a collision. An eye exam is the test that matters, because blue-and-tan silk still looks perfect when the retina is failing.</p>
<p>Tiny puppies drop into hypoglycemia if meals are skipped. Wobbling, a blank stare, and a body that goes limp are the signs I know from the treatment table. Grown dogs belong at 7 pounds or below, so the fuel reserve is already thin. A missed breakfast in a puppy that small is a medical event, and I keep the feeding times written where nobody can shrug them off.</p>`,

  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-temperament.html": `<p>Bold shows up the first time the doorbell rings and a dog about the size of a teapot plants all four feet. Yorkshire Terriers are toy terriers, and the terrier half is the half people underestimate. Mine squared up to a delivery cart, barked until the hallway echoed, and accepted a lap only after the porch was empty again. Barky is a fair word. The voice is sharp and ready, and it comes out for squirrels, footsteps, and a refrigerator door if the morning has been too quiet.</p>
<p>That courage sits inside a 7-pound maximum. A finished adult at 4 pounds carries the same opinions as a dog who uses every ounce of that 7. Size never talked mine out of supervising the door. What lowered the volume was a job small enough to finish: a sniff route, a stuffed toy to dissect, and a cue that paid silence after the first alarm bark. By age 3 the alarm is either a habit you can interrupt or a career. By age 5 an unpracticed Yorkie will announce every pigeon as if the building were under siege.</p>
<p>They bond hard and can be chilly with strangers until the introduction is slow. Guests at my place toss a treat sideways and keep hands off the top of the head until the dog steps in. Roughhousing with bigger dogs is a poor plan. The boldness will start a quarrel the body cannot finish. A single-dog home with a crate that stays private has matched every Yorkie I have taken off the clinic foster list.</p>
<p>Birthdays commonly land from 11 out to 15, and the mind stays a terrier through them. My oldest, photographed at 11 and still bossing the hallway at 15, used the same pitch on the mailman that he used as a youngster. Stamina changed. The opinions did not. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/yorkshire-terrier/">AKC page on the Yorkshire Terrier</a> describes that toy-terrier spark, and it matches the dog who supervises the house from a cushion.</p>`,

  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Part the hair down the spine and the grooming job is suddenly obvious. The coat is long and silky, traditionally parted down the back, steel blue on the body with tan on the head, chest, and legs. I find the midline from the occiput to the tail set with oiled fingertips and comb each side outward so the part stays straight. Skip that part for a weekend and the silk crosses the spine and dries into a rope.</p>
<p>A daily comb is the prevention plan. The coat mats to the skin when dead hair stays tangled against the body. I start at the feet with a fine metal comb, move up the legs, then the chest, then the ears, and I reach the back only after the comb teeth touch skin without snagging. A pea-sized knot in the armpit becomes a felted plate if it sits. On a dog at 4 pounds and on a dog at 7 pounds the method matches, because both carry more hair than body. Left alone for 3 days, silk behind the ears can felt to the leather. A neglected jacket has taken 5 hours at the salon in my log, the dog wrapped and dried in sections so the skin could breathe between passes.</p>
<p>The topknot stays out of the eyes with a small band or a wrap, changed whenever it slips, because a tight band rubs a bald spot. Tears stain the tan face fur. I wipe the eye corners with a damp cloth before the comb, then comb the beard so food cannot dry into cement. Hair grows inside the ear canal on many of these dogs. I pluck only the strands the clinic has already shown me, and I leave the rest, because a hard pull inflames the canal.</p>
<p>Baths are occasional and thorough. I wet to the skin, shampoo twice, rinse until the water runs clear, and squeeze the coat in a towel before a low dryer. High heat frizzes silk into fuzz and can overheat a dog this small. I comb while the coat is damp, then again when it is dry, resetting the part each time. A pet trim that leaves the body shorter and the face furnished still needs the comb, or the shorter ends mat closer to the skin where they are harder to see.</p>
<p>Dogs I kept in coat from year 11 through year 15 had a comb by the couch and a person who used it during the evening news. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/yorkshire-terrier/">Yorkshire Terrier coat description at AKC</a> is the picture I aim the part at: long, silky, steel blue and tan, divided down the back. The 7-pound ceiling still applies, and a 4-pound adult mats just as fast when that comb stays in a drawer.</p>`,

  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Rat-catching is still in the legs, even when the hunt is a baseboard and a squeaky. A Yorkshire Terrier can patrol a whole apartment before breakfast and still have opinions left. I build the week around that old vermin job. Monday is a sniff walk on a harness, slow enough that the nose actually works. Tuesday is a flirt toy dragged along the floor indoors, prey moving away, with a stop cue before the bark spiral starts.</p>
<p>Wednesday, day 3 on my chart, is the longer outing: a new block or a quiet park edge where smells are fresh and off-leash giants are absent. Thursday repeats the short sniff and adds a food scatter in a snuffle mat so the brain tires before the throat does. Friday, day 5, stays easy. A courtyard lap, a chew, and an early nap. Saturday brings the flirt toy back if the knees looked sound all week. Sunday is a lap and a window.</p>
<p>Rest belongs in the week because collapsing trachea and luxating patellas both complain when every outing is a sprint. A honking cough after a collar yank means the next walk is on a harness and slower. A skip in the hind end means the toy stays on the floor and jumps leave the menu. I have watched a Yorkie try to match a jogging human and come home coughing. The original work was bursts and pauses in a barn. The pauses are the part people drop.</p>
<p>Barking climbs when the hunt never happens. A dog who only circles the same hallway will announce the building out of boredom. Ten focused minutes of prey play have settled more of my evenings than an aimless extra loop. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/yorkshire-terrier">VCA's notes on Yorkshire Terriers</a> sit well beside that picture of a small terrier who still needs a purpose and a soft landing afterward.</p>`,

  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Miss a meal in the first weeks and a tiny Yorkshire Terrier puppy can fold from hypoglycemia. I set a timer and feed every 3 hours through those early days, then stretch the gap toward 5 hours only after the puppy stays bright, warm, and steady between bowls. Wobbling, a glassy stare, or a sudden slump is a clinic trip. A dab of honey on the gums is only the stopgap the vet on duty has already described for that specific puppy. The adult ceiling is 7 pounds, so reserves are already small.</p>
<p>The first year is mostly coat, teeth, and nerve. Baby teeth give way while the jacket is still the darker puppy silk, shorter than the adult fall of blue and tan. I run a few comb strokes at every meal so a part down the back becomes ordinary handling. The handling stays brief. A long lecture loses a terrier puppy, and a mat still starts if nobody touches the ears.</p>
<p>The folder comes out before any deposit. I want patella results on both parents, an eye exam noted in writing, and a straight answer about portosystemic shunt in the line. A litter where one pup stayed stunted and vacant after meals is a litter I leave. Housetraining is ordinary and frequent. Boldness shows early. A barky eight-week-old guarding a toy is telling the truth about the breed. Calm people and quiet dogs teach that courage manners. Dog-park chaos is where a toy body picks quarrels it cannot win. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/yorkshire-terrier">VCA Yorkshire Terrier puppy overview</a> is the handout I pair with that folder.</p>`,

  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-shedding-guide.html": `<p>The couch stays strangely clean, which fools people into leaving the comb in a drawer. A Yorkshire Terrier does shed. Hair lets go from the follicle and then stays caught in the long silky coat. The living room can look low-shed while a pelt of dead silk hides against the skin. I find it when I part the coat and the comb fills with strands that never reached the floor.</p>
<p>Friction collects the most: armpits, behind the ears, the groin, the underside of the tail, and anywhere a collar still rubs. On an adult at 4 pounds the felt builds fast because the distance from hair tip to skin is tiny. On an adult at 7 pounds the same physics cover a slightly wider chest. I comb on day 3 and day 5 even in a crowded week, and I comb the other days when my fingers feel a bump. Skip those passes and the shed hair mats to the skin.</p>
<p>Spring and fall loosen more fiber than midwinter, though the blow is milder than a herding coat. Body silk and tan furnishings both participate. Puppies drop the darker juvenile coat as adult color comes in, and that transition is when mats ambush someone who thought a puppy jacket could wait. Dogs I groomed from year 11 into year 15 shed the same way they did at two. Age changed the teeth and the airway more than the follicle.</p>
<p>Bathing without a pre-comb turns loose hair into felt. I line-comb, bathe, then comb the damp coat so shed strands rinse free. A sweater traps the same loose hair against the body and comes off for a combing before it goes back on. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/yorkshire-terrier/">AKC write-up of the Yorkie coat</a> is what I send when someone argues that a silky toy terrier needs no coat care because the sofa looks bare.</p>`,

  "guides/yorkshire-terrier-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>I quote 11 to 15 years, and I have the photos to keep me honest. Some Yorkshire Terriers in my albums are still supervising a hallway at the high end of that span. Others left nearer the low end when the mouth or the airway failed first. A frame capped at 7 pounds has less margin when something goes wrong. A senior at 4 pounds feels a dental infection in the whole dog.</p>
<p>Dental disease steals comfortable years more often than any dramatic emergency. Red gums, loose teeth, and a jaw that hurts make a dog stop eating while the birthday still looks far away. I want the dental chart started at year 3, while the face still looks young, because waiting for a foul smell spends the middle of that 11 to 15 year line. Luxating patellas and Legg-Calve-Perthes disease shrink the active part of life even when the calendar keeps going. A dog who can no longer jump still counts the years, and those years get smaller.</p>
<p>Collapsing trachea is the nighttime scare. A honking cough, a blue tinge during a bark fit, and outings that have to shrink are how the upper airway takes time away. A missed portosystemic shunt in puppyhood can remove a dog from the line entirely. Progressive retinal atrophy usually takes sight and leaves the years, and a blind Yorkie who knows the floor plan can still reach 15. Hypoglycemia is the puppy crisis, and it cuts life short when a tiny pup is left without food until the crash is severe.</p>
<p>At year 5 I repeat the airway listen, the stifle palpation, and the dental look, and I keep the scale from creeping over that 7-pound ceiling. Extra fat on a dog this small makes the trachea and the knees work harder. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/yorkshire-terrier">VCA lifespan context for Yorkshire Terriers</a> belongs in the same talk as the comb and the dental calendar, because mouth care and coat care are how a lot of these dogs actually arrive at the far birthday.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-yorkshire-terriers-get.html": `<p>Put the adult on a kitchen scale before you trust a guess. A Yorkshire Terrier must not exceed 7 pounds. That ceiling is the measurement I use, and it covers males and females. There is no separate taller column to memorize. The breed is a toy terrier. A finished dog at 4 pounds is inside the rule. A dog at 8 pounds has left it. I have watched people brag about a big one and then struggle with a kneecap and a trachea that were never built to haul the extra mass.</p>
<p>Puppies fool the eye because the coat arrives before the substance. At month 3 the body is a handful and the face is still tan fluff. At month 5 the legs look longer than they will, which is mostly puppy coat playing a trick. Most of the dogs I have weighed were near their adult pounds between month 8 and month 12. After month 12, added ounces are usually fat or hair, and the scale is how you separate them. A damp coat can hide a thin ribcage. Feel first, then believe the number.</p>
<p>The first year is also when hypoglycemia punishes a skipped meal, so growth and the bowl stay tied. Tiny pups need steady calories while they climb toward that adult ceiling. I record weight every week in a notebook. A puppy who stalls, acts vacant after eating, or falls far behind littermates needs a shunt conversation, because a portosystemic shunt can look like the one who is simply petite. I have lived with a female who settled at 4 pounds and took up an entire chair in attitude, and with a male who landed just under 7 and still had to respect the same ceiling.</p>
<p>Once the pounds are honest, birthdays commonly fall between 11 and 15. Crossing the ceiling does nothing kind for those birthdays. A weekly weigh-in on the same scale, morning and dry, keeps coat volume from masquerading as growth. Dental disease, slipping patellas, and collapsing trachea all get harder when the frame is heavier. Blue and tan silk, with the traditional part down the back, adds visual bulk without adding pounds. Comb it, then weigh. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/yorkshire-terrier/">AKC Yorkshire Terrier size notes</a> are the page I send to anyone hunting for a number the scale will refuse to support.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-health-issues.html": `<p>Herding all day means little if the hips will not hold the turns. In an Australian Shepherd, dysplastic hips show up as a bunny hop, a slow rise after a nap, and stiffness on the first passes across the yard. I ask for hip films once the skeleton is mature enough for the pictures to mean something, and I want the parents' results in hand before that. Eyes hide better. Collie eye anomaly can sit in an eye that looks ordinary from the driveway. Hereditary cataracts tied to HSF4 cloud the lens later. The owner then sees bumping, a bluish cast, or a missed treat at dusk. An eye exam plus HSF4 results are the pair I ask to see.</p>
<p>MDR1 drug-sensitivity mutation belongs on the clinic chart before anyone reaches for a parasite product. I have watched the scary version start as stumbling and wide pupils after a medication the family thought was routine. Tell the hospital the MDR1 status at intake. Epilepsy here is the seizure disorder that arrives without a tidy cause. A dog who is ordinary at breakfast and convulsing by noon needs a workup, a written log of episodes, and a plan that keeps stairs off-limits during recovery.</p>
<p>Merle is an allowed pattern, and it is also a mating hazard. Breeding merle to merle can produce deaf and blind puppies. I ask to see both parents in good light, or their color genotypes, before I trust a litter ad. A homozygous merle pup may show odd eyes, a largely white coat, and a missed startle. That welfare problem is created at the mating, which is why a double-merle sales pitch ends my visit.</p>
<p>I put dates on the follow-up: hip films discussed by year 3, eyes looked at again at year 5, sooner if a cloud or a limp appears. A seizure log with the clock times written in the margin saves the next visit from guesswork. The <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC orthopedic and eye filings for Australian Shepherds</a> are where I want those papers to live, so a buyer is reading results.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-temperament.html": `<p>A quiet afternoon feels like an accusation to an Australian Shepherd. This is a herding dog that wants a job, and the job can be stock, a flirt pole, kibble scattered in tall grass, or the family moving from the car to the door. The dog who shared my house inventoried everyone each evening, circling until the whole family stood in the room he had decided was the pen. When the inventory failed, he invented work: shoes piled in a corner, then a stare until I acknowledged the gather.</p>
<p>Heel nipping is that same job aimed at ankles. The dog tracks motion from behind and puts teeth on a calf to push the moving thing the right way. It hurts, and it is purposeful. I interrupt it the week it appears, trade the nip for a toy at chest height, and give a legal gather so the urge has a destination. Walking away while scolding teaches that the nip worked, because the target moved. Stop your feet, wait for a sit, then go again. Children need that pattern coached. A running child is the most convincing sheep in the house.</p>
<p>I measure effort in minutes the brain can use. A 40-minute session of gathers, stops, and flanks beats an undefined jog. If the dog is still locking eyes on movement at minute 65, the task was too vague and the arousal is still up. Day 3 at my house is the harder session, long line and a moving toy standing in for stock. Day 5 is easier on purpose: a sniff walk and a settle on a mat. A herding mind that never powers down becomes a night barker and a shadow chaser.</p>
<p>Strangers get a pause, then a decision. Early introductions to people, bikes, livestock, and other dogs keep that pause from hardening into suspicion. When joints and eyes cooperate, the years I plan often run 12 to 15. Work drive at 12 can look like work drive at 2. Recovery fades first, which is why the 40-minute job should end while the dog can still think. Merle markings change the picture people see. A red merle and a black tri can share the same need to control motion. I judge heels, eye contact, and whether a settle is possible after the gather. Color never told me which dog would quit when the sheep stopped. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/australian-shepherd/">AKC account of Australian Shepherd character</a> lines up with the dog who is happiest when the day contains a task with a finish.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Hands go to the pants first on an Australian Shepherd. The double coat carries a harsher guard layer over a softer undercoat, and the feathers on the backs of the thighs collect burrs and loose wool before the saddle shows anything. I set the dog on a nonslip mat, mist the feathering, and comb from the skin outward, then follow with a rake that reaches undercoat without scraping hide.</p>
<p>A shed week is a timed chore in my bathroom. I block 40 minutes, pull tufts from the britches, the chest, and behind the ears, and I have counted past 65 loose clumps when the coat was blowing. Guard hairs stay. Undercoat is what lets go. Bathing in the middle of a blow, before the rake, felts that wool into the guard coat. Rake first, bathe second, rake again while the dog is damp.</p>
<p>Day 3 of an ordinary week is a pin-brush pass over the saddle and the neck. Day 5 is the feathering and the tail, where debris hides. Other evenings I run a palm over the hips and deal with any tangle while it is still small. Floppy ear leather holds damp, so I wipe it and comb the fringe underneath until it dries. Feet collect grass seeds between the pads, and those get checked every time the comb is out.</p>
<p>The heavy sheds I remember landed around month 12 in a young dog and again with the seasons after that. A dog I kept to year 15 still dropped a bucket of undercoat each spring. The span from 12 to 15 was the same chore with a grayer muzzle. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/australian-shepherd">VCA grooming picture for the Australian Shepherd</a> matches what the rake finds: a double coat that sheds in volume when the undercoat releases.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Stock work is the template, even when the stock is a long-line toy and a set of cones. An Australian Shepherd wants a job with turns, stops, and a reason to watch movement. I lay the week out so the herding urge gets a container and the body gets a day that is genuinely quiet.</p>
<p>Monday is flank practice in the yard. The dog moves wide of the toy, sits on a word, and gets paid for the stop. Tuesday is a sniff walk where pulling is dull and a glance back is worth something. Wednesday, marked day 3 on the fridge, is the big session, up to 40 minutes of gathers if the footing is grass and the dog is already conditioned. I stop earlier when the tongue is a spoon and the turns get sloppy. Thursday repeats a smaller flank game. Friday is day 5, a decompression walk and a mat settle, with chase games done before dusk. Saturday can stretch. I have let a conditioned adult work toward 65 minutes of mixed drills and walking, and I have also stopped at the first uneven stride. Sunday is off. A herding dog who works every day with no down day gets brittle.</p>
<p>Heel nipping shows up on the walk home if the outing had distance and no decisions. I give one last tiny assignment, a carried leash tab or a search for keys, so the brain closes the loop. Rest days spare hips that may be heading toward trouble, and they spare human ankles. Puppies use the same shapes with the clock cut hard. Growth plates and hours of forced outruns are a bad pair. The adult 40-minute job waits until the body is built.</p>
<p>Across a life that often runs 12 to 15 years, the week shrinks before the desire does. At 12 I keep the job and drop the duration. At 15 I keep the eye contact and the stop, and the gathers become a few strides. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/australian-shepherd/">AKC notes on Australian Shepherd work drive</a> remind me that a thinking dog is the point. Empty circles still leave the dog unemployed.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The first year with an Australian Shepherd is a herding dog under construction. Height is what the registry prints: 20 to 23 inches on males and 18 to 21 inches on females. A puppy who looks huge at four months is often just leg. Jumping, twisted fetches, and long forced runs stay off the menu while those inches fill in. The energy goes into manners that will matter when the excuses run out.</p>
<p>Heel nipping can start as soon as the puppy notices that people move. Teeth on skin end the motion. A toy in the mouth restarts it. By month 3 the trade should feel boring. By month 5 it should hold when a child runs, which means the child has practiced too. A pup allowed to herd a toddler for laughs becomes a ten-month-old who bruises calves.</p>
<p>Money stays in my pocket until the folder is real. I want MDR1 status, an eye report that addresses collie eye anomaly, HSF4 results for hereditary cataracts, and a hip-evaluation plan for the parents. Merle patterning is permitted on this breed. Pairing two merles can throw pups who cannot hear or see, so both parents' color genetics need to be in writing. A mostly white puppy with a shy startle needs a hearing check. A discount paired with a promise is how that puppy gets sent home anyway.</p>
<p>Socialization covers livestock, surfaces, sounds, and strangers while the puppy is still easy to lift. The stare at motion is already the job showing up. That pup needs a legal outlet and a crate that means the workday is over. I practice the crate while the puppy is still glad to nap, before the herding brain decides the crate is an insult. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/australian-shepherd">VCA overview for Australian Shepherd puppies</a> is the sheet I set beside the test results.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-shedding-guide.html": `<p>The hair on the floor comes out of the undercoat. An Australian Shepherd wears a double coat: longer guard hairs that stay anchored, and a softer undercoat that releases in clumps when the season shifts or the indoor heat stays high. I can point at the source with a rake. Behind the ears, along the belly, under the collar area, and in the breeching on the thighs, wool packs in and then lets go. The saddle sheds too, though those hairs are shorter and sneak into fabric before a tumbleweed appears.</p>
<p>Spring and fall are the loud months. One weekend can pull enough undercoat to fill a grocery bag, and the couch still shows more the next morning because the release continues after the session. I card the coat on day 3 of a blow and again on day 5, and I vacuum the dog bed both times. Between blows the shed is a steady sprinkle of guard hair plus a little wool, enough to notice on black pants.</p>
<p>Bathing locks the story in place. Wet undercoat that was already loose mats into the guard layer if nobody rakes first. I rake, bathe, dry with airflow down to the skin, and rake once more. A slicker used only on the surface polishes the outside and leaves the shedding layer stuck underneath. The dog looks finished and the floor still grows a pelt by dinner. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/australian-shepherd/">AKC description of the Australian Shepherd coat</a> is the double coat I am always trying to reach at skin level.</p>`,

  "guides/australian-shepherd-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Plan on 12 to 15 years with an Australian Shepherd who stays sound. I have celebrated 12 with a dog who still wanted stock, and I have combed gray faces at 15 who had retired to a mat by the door and still tracked everything that moved. The line is long enough that the things which shorten it have time to matter.</p>
<p>Bad hips from hip dysplasia take dogs out of work in the middle years. A dog who cannot finish a gather, who bunny-hops, or who stalls on stairs loses the life the breed was built for. Pain shrinks the pleasant part of those 12 to 15 years even while the heart keeps beating. Epilepsy cuts in more abruptly. Cluster seizures, falls, and the recovery days steal ordinary weeks, and a severe course can end a dog well before that span. Collie eye anomaly from early life, and hereditary cataracts linked to HSF4 later, take the work away when a dog can no longer read movement. Blindness may leave the birthdays intact and still erase the job.</p>
<p>MDR1 drug-sensitivity mutation can end a life in a single afternoon if the wrong drug is given. That one is preventable when the result sits on the chart. Pairing two merles can leave puppies deaf, blind, or both, and that start is already compromised. I put hip films on the calendar by year 3 and an eye recheck at year 5 so the middle of the span is a decision with the pictures in hand. Families who only meet the dog on good days miss the seizure diary and the morning stiffness that actually spend the years. A written log beside the food bin has changed more of my clinic talks than any brochure. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/australian-shepherd">VCA notes on Australian Shepherd longevity</a> belong next to those dates.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-australian-shepherds-get.html": `<p>Inches are the registry figures, and I quote them before anyone quotes a pound guess. Male Australian Shepherds stand 20 to 23 inches. Females stand 18 to 21 inches at the shoulder. A male who measures 23 is at the top of the box. A female who measures 18 is at the bottom and still correct. I recheck the tape after the coat is smoothed flat, because a lifted neck can steal half an inch. I measure at the withers with the dog standing square. Neck coat adds fake height, and a crouch hides a real one.</p>
<p>Filling out takes longer than toy-breed people expect. I watch height settle between month 10 and month 16. At month 10 plenty of males are all leg and a narrow chest. By month 16 the sternum has dropped, the thigh has widened, and the lanky teenager starts to look like a herding dog. I recheck body condition at month 3 and month 5 so a heavy youngster gets caught while growth plates are still the priority. A puppy stuffed to look impressive, or kept gaunt for a photo, both miss a dog who lands inside those inch ranges with enough muscle to work.</p>
<p>Pound chatter wanders through the clinic. I have seen 40 on a young male's sheet while he was still growing upward, and I have seen 65 on a mature dog with a deep chest. I do not hand those two figures out as a standard. Height is the figure I hand out. A heavy dog will feel it in the hips, and dysplastic hips are why the scale still matters when the registry spoke in inches. Feel ribs under the double coat. The coat lies.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/australian-shepherd/">AKC height lines for Australian Shepherds</a> are what I want a puppy contract to echo: males 20 to 23, females 18 to 21, with month 10 through month 16 as the window when the outline becomes honest.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-health-issues.html": `<p>Hearts come first, before kisses and coat color. Mitral valve disease is the adult heart problem the parent club treats as expected screening for these toy spaniels. At home the early picture is a new cough, faster breathing on the couch, tiring on a route that used to be easy, or a dog who waits to be lifted. In the exam room the picture is a murmur. I want the parents screened and the note written down, and I want this dog's chest heard while the muzzle is still dark. A spaniel at 13 pounds and a spaniel at 18 pounds can both grow that murmur. Pounds fail to sort the valve.</p>
<p>Syringomyelia with Chiari-like malformation is the neck and brain problem. The sign families finally name is phantom scratching: a hind foot paddling at the neck or shoulder while the skin stays untouched, sometimes with a yelp when someone lifts the dog or the collar shifts. Neck pain can look like a refusal of the harness or sleep with the chin propped up. Episodic falling is a separate event. The dog stiffens, collapses, or moves in a strange stiff gait, then stands again. I ask for the DNA test for episodic falling on both parents so the folder separates that disease from a clumsy puppy.</p>
<p>Dry eye and curly coat arrive together in the unlucky pup: a harsh curly jacket plus eyes that are painful and short on tears. An eye check plus a coat that feels wrong for silk are the clues. A slipping kneecap announces itself as a hitch in the hind step. Hip trouble looks like a sway, or like a long pause before hopping onto the sofa. Both belong on the orthopedic list beside the heart notes. Height here is 12 to 13 inches, easy to forget once the dog lives in a lap.</p>
<p>I have heard murmurs in a Cavalier still playing at 9 and in another who tired on one flight of stairs at 14. Those are individual dogs, and they are why a gray muzzle is the wrong time to start listening. Book the listen during year 3 and keep it scheduled during year 5, then at visits after that. People hope for a band of 12 to 15 years, and heart disease shortens many of those years. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck's veterinary manual on mitral valve disease</a> is the clinical frame I hand over for the cough and the murmur, without turning one spaniel into a made-up rate.</p>
<p>Film the air scratch and film a falling spell, because both are brief and an appointment can land between them. Bring the clips with the parents' heart notes. A gentle toy spaniel will keep greeting you while a valve leaks, which is why the stethoscope has to notice first. Phantom scratching gets waved off as itch until someone watches the foot miss the neck. Falling gets waved off as a trip on a rug. The phone clip is how those two stay different in the record.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-temperament.html": `<p>A lap is both a place and a career. Cavalier King Charles Spaniels are toy spaniels, gentle and people-focused in the specific way of following you from the sink to the mailbox and sighing when the chair empties. Mine treated every return as a reunion after a voyage, including the trip that only took the trash to the curb. The softness is real. It also means time alone needs a rehearsal, or the people-focus becomes a panicked soundtrack against the door.</p>
<p>Alone-time starts around month 3, a short spell behind a gate while the puppy is already tired, and it lengthens slowly. Near month 5 a puppy who has always had company in the room will melt down at the first real errand. Guest manners matter even when the greeting is sweet. Mine launched the whole body, paws included, because contact was the point. A sit that earns the hello keeps the spaniel welcome with people who are unsteady on their feet.</p>
<p>Blenheim, tricolor, ruby, and black and tan are coat colors, and none of them predict the velcro. Play is affectionate. A tossed toy comes back because you are inside the game. Work that sends the dog away holds less interest than work done at your knee. Children on the floor do better than children who squeeze. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel/">AKC portrait of Cavalier King Charles Spaniel temperament</a> matches the dog who would rather stay in contact than collect titles.</p>
<p>Separation practice fails when it only happens on a day you are already late. I leave a stuffed food toy, step out, and return before the cry becomes the lesson. The crate sits in the lived-in room and stays open otherwise, so it reads as a den. A spaniel sent to a far bedroom learns that distance is the emergency. Gentle dogs can still be loud about loneliness. The volume mixes grief and demand, and answering only at the peak teaches the peak.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Silky ears are where the grooming on this toy spaniel actually happens. Feathering is soft, and it mats behind the ears before anywhere else, then in the armpits, the trousers, and the tail. I sit the dog between my knees, fold one ear leather back, and comb from the tips toward the skull until metal reaches skin. A knot left in that fringe holds damp against the leather and smells sour by tomorrow. Those ears get combed when the weekday number is 3 and again when it is 5, plus any morning the fringe looks twisted.</p>
<p>Color tells me where debris hides. Blenheim is chestnut on white, and tears rust the blaze. Tricolor puts black into that same map. Ruby is rich red, so specks show as dark dots in the feathering. Black and tan hides tear stain on the face and shows every pale strand on the saddle. I wipe the eyes before the comb so rust stays off the muzzle. Pad hair mats into pebbles. I trim only the bit that collects ice and grit, and I leave the leg furnishings that make the outline read as a spaniel.</p>
<p>The curly-coat dry-eye pairing changes the feel in the hands. That puppy wears a harsh curl, and the eyes need the clinic's tear plan. The coat will refuse to comb like salon silk. Ordinary Cavaliers have the silk. I bathe when feathers smell or pollen cakes the trousers, wetting through, conditioning the lengths, and drying with airflow while I comb so the ear fringe cannot lock into a rope. Eyes stay visible. A spaniel who cannot see your face loses the contact that makes the breed easy company.</p>
<p>Nails click sooner than the feathered foot suggests. I take a few at a time while the dog is already in the grooming sit. Teeth are a later evening with a finger brush, because feathering around the muzzle still hides tartar. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel">VCA coat-care notes on this spaniel's silk</a> line up with an ear-first routine that keeps silk from felting where the cushion rubs.</p>
<p>I keep a spare comb in the car. Lap dogs get held, and holding presses the ear fringe flat for the whole ride. A two-minute comb in a parking spot before you go inside saves the felt that would otherwise greet you at bedtime. Chest feathering collects food. I lift it and comb downward after meals when the dog is a messy drinker, which many of them are.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Company on a walk is the original spaniel job. A course that runs until someone drops belongs to a herding breed, and it is the wrong week for this spaniel. I keep outings gentle and I change them the moment the chest or the neck complains. Whether the scale says 13 pounds or it says 18 pounds, the map can stay the same. What changes is how fast pavement heats and how soon a murmur, if one exists, shows up as lagging.</p>
<p>Monday and Tuesday are neighborhood strolls with sniffing allowed, harness fitted, pace slow enough for conversation. The middle outing, the one I mark 3, is a longer loop in cool air, still flat if the dog is older. Thursday is a soft retrieve indoors, several tosses, then a stop while interest remains. The quiet outing, marked 5, is a courtyard wander, a chew, and time in a lap. Saturday repeats the longer stroll only if breathing looked easy. Sunday stays restful with people nearby. Shutting a door on this dog fails as rest, because the whole project is proximity.</p>
<p>Hills shrink when a 9-year-old starts coughing, breathing harder while lying still, or asking to turn around. A companion I walked at 14 stayed on level ground and still got the morning outing, because the point was the person and the nose. Hopeful years of 12 to 15 are often cut back by mitral valve disease. Phantom scratching or neck pain changes the gear too. A harness that presses a sore neck gets swapped, and the toy stays low so the head stays level.</p>
<p>Those neurologic signs, and any murmur, mean a call to the clinic before distance grows. Episodic falling, when it is this dog's diagnosis, means the week skips the excited burst that triggers a collapse. Even a Cavalier without those labels needs the off day. Slipping kneecaps and hips that may be dysplastic make stair repeats a poor fitness idea. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel">VCA exercise context covering these spaniels</a> fits a spaniel whose work is company, kept inside what the chest and the neck can pay for.</p>
<p>Heat edits the map on its own. Summer midday loops become early loops, and the lap replaces a second walk when panting looks like labor. I count resting breaths when a cough is new, write the number, and bring it along. Enthusiasm at the door is a spaniel being a spaniel. It is weak evidence that the heart is fine.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The folder arrives before the puppy does. The deposit on this spaniel waits on heart notes. This breed's expected screen is mitral valve disease, the valve problem clubs already treat as routine in adults. I also want episodic-falling DNA results, patella results, and hip results. Curly coat plus dry eyes in a litter stops me. A harsh curly jacket with dry, uncomfortable eyes is a named condition, and it is visible if you handle the pups instead of watching a clip.</p>
<p>Growth in the opening year heads toward 12 to 13 inches in height, while weight heads for 13 to 18 pounds. Silk replaces the puppy coat along the way. I weigh at week 9 and again at week 14 so a stall prompts a question while the breeder is still answering. During month 3 I recheck body and bite and begin real alone-time practice. During month 5 the baby teeth are mostly gone and the feathering behind the ears needs a comb whenever the puppy is lifted. Mats begin as a tiny knot.</p>
<p>People-focus is present immediately. Gentle handling, laps, and visitors who let the puppy approach build a spaniel you can take into ordinary rooms. Rough parks stay out of this year. A toy body that may carry a slipping kneecap gains nothing from being tumbled. Air-scratching toward the neck, or pain on lifting, gets filmed and phoned in. Those signs are the home face of syringomyelia with Chiari-like malformation, and a clip beats a vague memory.</p>
<p>Meals stay on a clock. A run of skipped bowls helps nobody at this size. Late in the year a sound pup is moving into an adult range of 13 to 18 pounds, with the withers around 12 to 13. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC papers to read before a Cavalier deposit</a> are the orthopedic half of the folder. Heart notes sit with them, and both get read before any cash moves.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Shed hair on this toy spaniel leaves the follicle and then hangs in the silky coat. Fine strands come from the ear fringe, the chest, the backs of the legs, the tail, and the slight wave over the ribs. They rarely sail across a room the way undercoat does. They tangle into live hair, especially where an ear rubs a cushion, and they become the mats behind the ears. A sofa that looks nearly clean has simply not been interrogated. The interrogation tool is the comb.</p>
<p>Color makes the source obvious. On Blenheim, chestnut hairs show in the white ear fringe and white hairs show on dark clothes. Tricolor adds black strands that glitter on a pale couch. Ruby sheds red into every fabric, and the volume is easier to underestimate because the dog is one color. Black and tan drops pale hairs onto the dark saddle, where a lazy glance skips. Hind-leg trousers are a reservoir. Sit the dog and the shed silk is woven into the longer feather under your hand.</p>
<p>Weekday 3 and weekday 5 are my minimum comb days. Heavier weeks get a morning pass as well. I start at the ear base, lift the fringe, and comb outward so loose hair exits the coat. A bath that skips the pre-comb rinses some shed hair away and felts the remainder. Afterward I comb while damp. Harnesses and sweaters grind loose hair into the armpit, so the gear comes off and that patch is combed before anything goes back on.</p>
<p>Season shifts loosen more silk, and so does the move from puppy coat into adult feathering. A youngster can seem like a light shedder until the trousers lengthen and the broom finds rolls of color. Seniors keep shedding. The follicle stays on the job when the heart gets louder, which is a grooming fact and a medical fact together. Vacuuming the bed and skipping the dog continues the cycle. Ear fringe rests on a pillow for hours, drops strands, and mats what remains. One comb lives on my nightstand for that reason.</p>
<p>Tail feathering sheds onto the clothes of whoever is holding the dog. People-focus means the evening is spent against fabric, so the silk has a destination. The destination can be the comb. It can also be a couch seam. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel">VCA shedding notes for the Cavalier coat</a> agree with what a full comb shows: the hair was on the dog, caught in the furnishings, the whole time.</p>`,

  "guides/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>I quote 12 to 15 years for these spaniels, and the next breath names mitral valve disease, which shortens many of those lives. I lost one at 9 to heart failure, a dog who coughed after dark and tired on a single flight of stairs. I also combed a spaniel who was still soft-eyed and lap-bound at 14, slower outdoors and still delighted by a key in the lock. Both memories sit inside that same 12 to 15. Heart disease is why many miss the far end.</p>
<p>Before goodbye, the home picture is often a murmur caught early, a cough, restlessness after midnight, or breathing that costs more effort while the dog sleeps. Syringomyelia with Chiari-like malformation can shrink comfort through neck pain and phantom scratching even while the birthday stays on the calendar. Episodic falling, slipping kneecaps, hip trouble, and dry eye with curly coat each remove a piece of ease. When someone asks why a gentle spaniel's years came up short, the heart is the answer I give.</p>
<p>Listening during year 3, and keeping that listen on the calendar during year 5, is how a murmur gets caught while the dog still climbs up beside you. Staying near 13 to 18 pounds, height close to 12 to 13 inches, protects nothing about the valve. Screening does that work, together with a clinic that treats a fresh cough as news the week it starts. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel">VCA perspective on Cavalier King Charles Spaniel years</a> is the page I pair with the stethoscope habit.</p>
<p>Gain on the scale and skipped listens spend the same years from different directions. I keep ribs findable under the feathering and I write a respiratory rate when rest breathing looks faster. A spaniel who still greets you can be in failure. The greeting is temperament. The cough is disease. Waiting for the dog to act miserable waits past the useful stretch of the 12 to 15 year line.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-cavalier-king-charles-spaniels-get.html": `<p>Height and weight both have a printed home. Adult Cavalier King Charles Spaniels stand 12 to 13 inches and weigh 13 to 18 pounds. Feathering gets smoothed, then I read height at the shoulder, because ear fringe and chest silk make a small spaniel look taller in a photograph than on a wicket. Males and females share that inch band and that pound band. Chasing a separate male column wastes the afternoon.</p>
<p>Much of the height arrives from month 8 to month 12. At month 8 the head can look finished while the chest is still narrow. By month 12 a lot of the height is set, and what remains is rib spring, muscle, and feather. I weigh again at month 9 and at month 14. Month 9 catches a puppy racing ahead of a destination set at 13 to 18 pounds. Month 14 catches the late fill that softens a lean yearling if the bowl never changed.</p>
<p>The opening year should land near those adult figures. Feel ribs through the silk. A spaniel who looks big-boned past the top of the range, say at 20 pounds, has left the 18-pound end, and the lap-dog frame carries that mass into the years when mitral valve disease is already the screening priority. Condition gets another look in year 3, then a fresh look once year 5 arrives, when feathering is heavy and eyes lie about the waist.</p>
<p>Blenheim, tricolor, ruby, or black and tan adds zero inches. A curly-coated puppy with dry eye is a health finding. That coat can look different while the goal stays 12 to 13 inches together with 13 to 18 pounds. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/cavalier-king-charles-spaniel/">AKC size lines for the Cavalier King Charles Spaniel</a> are the figures I copy onto the puppy chart and match to the scale from month 8 through month 12, then once more at month 14.</p>
<p>Food volume keeps the chart honest. I use one cup, one time of day, and a hand on the ribs every Sunday. Feathering can outpace muscle, and a fluffy outline gets praised while the scale climbs past 18. Save the praise for the number. A dog kept between 13 and 18 pounds, with a withers height of 12 to 13 inches, is the dog the registry described. I write the Sunday weight in the same notebook as the withers reading so a fluffy week cannot masquerade as a growth spurt. The cup stays level. Praise waits for the number on the page.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-health-issues.html": `<p>Big bones make people ignore a limp until the limp becomes the lifestyle. In a Cane Corso, dysplastic hips announce themselves with a sway, trouble climbing into the car, and a slow push up from a hard floor. I want hip evaluations on the parents and films on the dog once age 1 arrives and again by age 2, when the pictures say more than a puppy pose. Eyes fail in ways visible from across the room. Cherry eye is a red gland at the inner corner. Entropion rolls a lid inward so lashes scrape, and the dog squints and waters. Ectropion rolls the lid outward and leaves conjunctiva showing. Book the eye evaluation early. A guardian who hurts in the face gets sharper about having that face touched.</p>
<p>Bloat refuses to wait for office hours. The picture is restlessness, unproductive retching, and an abdomen that swells and feels drum-tight. The stomach may be twisting, and the drive is immediate. Idiopathic epilepsy is the seizure problem that remains when the workup finds no cause. During a seizure the dog needs space, a dim quiet room afterward, and a note of how long it lasted. Heart disease, including cardiomyopathy in some lines, shows up as weakness, coughing, fainting, or a rhythm someone notices only because they listened. I have recorded 88 for a lean female and 120 for a heavy male on charts in the same month, and neither figure shielded the heart or the stomach.</p>
<p>Years often fall between 9 and 12. Bloat, cardiomyopathy, and ruined hips are what make the low end real. A steady, socialized guardian can still be fragile inside. Registry height for males is 25 to 27.5 inches. Registry height for females is 23.5 to 26. I trust those heights more than kitchen rumors about mass. AKC recognition arrived in 2010, recent enough that eyes, hearts, and hips still vary by line. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck's pages on bloat and cardiomyopathy</a> are the references worth knowing before midnight needs them.</p>
<p>After a seizure, stairs and other pets wait until the mind returns. A lid rolled inward hurts on every blink. A prolapsed gland dries and swells if it stays out. Films from 1 to 2 years become the baseline when the dog is 9 and reluctant to rise, inside that 9 to 12 year stretch. The scale numbers I have actually handled, 88 and 120, go in the margin. Care plans follow joints, stomach, and heart. A red mass at the eye or a fade on a brief walk outranks gloss in the order I use.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-temperament.html": `<p>Guardian work is the whole dog. A Cane Corso is an Italian mastiff-type, and the picture needs a confident handler from the first week, someone who stays calm while a large dog leans, studies a new person, and decides whether that person belongs. Early socialization means many ordinary outings while you can still relocate the puppy. One class plus optimism leaves the sidewalk looking dangerous. Meeting nobody beyond the household teaches that the sidewalk itself is hostile.</p>
<p>AKC recognized the breed in 2010. The date matters because many owners met a finished image, a watchful mastiff type, and skipped the teaching behind it. Mine needed a job with edges: a bed by the door, a release word before greetings, and follow-through every single time. Suspicion of strangers can be quiet and steady. The failure mode is a dog who blocks guests, adds pressure each month, and has never been required to give up space.</p>
<p>Outings began in week 1 and ran through month 2, then weekly after that, with food, distance, and a departure when the puppy looked flooded. The Corso in my life stood at 88 pounds on a first birthday and at 120 pounds once the chest filled. Manners mattered more at 9 months than they did at 12 months. Decisions can still shift at 9 months. By 12 months the guardian habit is already sorting who may pass. Waiting for a dog to settle is how a family loses the ability to walk past a neighbor.</p>
<p>A span of 9 to 12 years means the manners have to survive the whole stretch. The handler the dog can read uses clear starts and stops, puts thinking inside the walks, and allows rest that is truly rest. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/cane-corso/">AKC history and temperament notes for the Cane Corso</a> state the guardian job plainly, including the 2010 recognition, and they match the dog who watches a doorway because the doorway is their post.</p>
<p>Children and guests inherit the handler's rules. Faces stay uncrowded. A block at the threshold gets no laugh and no retreat that rewards it. I rehearse doorways until passing a person is dull. Saturday socialization at a shop, with six days of window rehearsal in between, loses to the window. The daily view is the practice that sticks when outings stay rare.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Short hair still wants hands on it. With a Cane Corso I take a rubber curry and a hound glove, follow the lay from neck to tail, then go against it just enough to lift loose coat. The barrel gets 1 minute on an easy day. Heavy shed days take 2 minutes plus a damp cloth, because the hairs stick to skin and to dark cloth the moment they leave the follicle.</p>
<p>Whether the dog on the table is 88 pounds or 120 pounds, the shine comes from lifting that loose coat, and trousers still collect a film. Any facial fold is wiped and dried completely so moisture has nowhere to linger. Ears get inspected and the visible surface is cleaned gently. Nails on a tall dog click, then split, when they are postponed, so they are done while the glove is already in use. Soap stays rare. Mud gets a rinse, a full dry, and the glove again.</p>
<p><a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cane-corso">VCA short-coat notes on Cane Corsos</a> match the short jacket I can finish with those two tools. Reach is part of the chore: males reach 25 to 27.5 inches while females reach 23.5 to 26. You are working a tall back, and a sore human shoulder helps nobody.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A patrol with decisions in it, plus a place command that can end the patrol, is the guardian week. Miles without a brain are the lesser gift for a Cane Corso. On day 1 I use a structured route: heel on the busy stretch, sniffing on the quiet stretch, and a down beside a bench while people pass at a distance the dog can manage. Day 2 is briefer, scent games in the yard and a single calm hello with someone already known. Later days alternate those shapes. One day is fully off so sleep is real.</p>
<p>Jumps, sudden brakes, and pounding repeats wait through 1 to 2 years while joints finish. Bloat risk splits meals from hard work. Feed, pause, and keep the intense piece away from a full belly. When idiopathic epilepsy is already diagnosed, the week respects triggers the clinic named for that individual, heat among them. Fading on a walk, a cough, or a collapse ends the session and starts a heart discussion. Cardiomyopathy belongs in that discussion for some lines.</p>
<p>Young dogs keep meeting new things on these routes, beside a handler who can leave. Watching and deciding were the original assignment, so quiet watching is on the schedule. Locomotion is the other piece, and families overdo it. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/cane-corso/">AKC description of Cane Corso guardian work</a> is the job I try to house, rest included, inside one week.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Payment waits on a boring, complete folder. For a Cane Corso puppy I want parental hip clearances, eye notes covering cherry eye, entropion, and ectropion, and whatever cardiac work the line already did. Cardiomyopathy appears in some lines. Learning at the point of sale that nobody listened to a chest is an expensive kind of surprise. I ask directly about idiopathic epilepsy in the relatives. Cute footage will never show bloat. The adult risk still belongs in the conversation with the breeder.</p>
<p>Year one is rapid growth on a big frame. Males are headed for 25 to 27.5 inches, with females headed for 23.5 to 26 inches. Youngsters I have followed passed 88 pounds ahead of a first birthday and kept filling toward 120 as the chest dropped. Those are single animals, useful only as a warning about how fast the leash starts to matter. Height is still the number I repeat. Stairs, slick footing, and forced mileage mix badly with the growth. Hard impact stays parked until 1 to 2 years have gone by.</p>
<p>Social teaching starts while carrying is still possible. Each week adds people, surfaces, noise, and stable dogs, next to a handler who stays even. Guardian instinct without those weeks becomes fear wearing muscle. At 9 months the goal is a mat hold while a stranger passes, because you asked. At 12 months the same skill has to survive a heavier body. After that, 9 to 12 years is a common line, so the early manners are a long bet.</p>
<p>A 2010 AKC recognition date finishes nothing in the kitchen. The puppy still needs that confident handler and weekly outings across the whole opening year. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC hip filings to request on Cane Corso parents</a> are the hip page I finish reading before cash changes hands.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Short hairs leave a Cane Corso as separate little spears from the close coat all over the body. Each follicle releases a stiff strand easy to overlook on the dog and obvious on a black sofa, a seat, or fleece. Woolly clumps are somebody else's breed. What you get here is a steady sprinkle, thicker in spring and in fall, when more strands let go together and grab fabric.</p>
<p>The curry proves the address of the hair. Drag it over neck, shoulder, ribs, and thigh and it loads up with coat that was already loose. Belly and hind-leg backs donate to bedding because they are the lying surface. This happens at 88 pounds and it happens at 120 pounds. More skin, more follicles, and a bigger dog blankets more cushion each nap. Strands also work into a damp cheek fold.</p>
<p>Most evenings the glove runs for 1 minute. In the heavy season it runs for 2 minutes. Skip the curry and a bath sends some loose hair down the drain while the rest dries stuck. A damp follow-up wipe collects what the curry raised. Rollers clean the couch and ignore the skin. The skin is where tomorrow's glitter is growing.</p>
<p>Count on the sprinkle across the usual 9 to 12 years. Year 9 still looks like a short-coat shed. Year 12 is the same hair on a dog who may climb the sofa more carefully and then cover it anyway. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cane-corso">VCA notes on the Cane Corso's short coat</a> match what I pull out of the car after a single errand.</p>
<p>Dark shirts advertise pale tips, and pale shirts advertise black ones. The glove by the door, used before bed privileges, cuts the overnight scatter. On seasonal weeks the vacuum happens the same day as the curry, or the floor looks innocent until you sit. Innocent is a trick of size. The hairs are small. They are plentiful. I wipe the car seat before a passenger in a black coat sits down, because one ride is enough to outline a hip on the cushion. That outline is the whole shedding lesson.</p>`,

  "guides/cane-corso-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The planning number I use for a Cane Corso is 9 to 12 years. Mastiff-type dogs often sit on the shorter side of the wider canine map, and this breed follows them. I have known watchful dogs at the high end, and I have known dogs gone near 9 after restlessness and retching opened into a crisis.</p>
<p>Bloat spends years in a handful of hours. The event is sudden. Unproductive retching plus a tight abdomen are the signs, and the chance of coming home depends on reaching a surgeon fast. Cardiomyopathy and other heart disease spend the span through weakness, fainting, cough, and collapse. Bad hips spend the comfortable portion by making standing and car entry painful, even if the calendar still completes. Idiopathic epilepsy adds injuries and, on a grim course, an earlier end. A prolapsed tear gland, a lid rolled in, or a lid rolled out is miserable and often fixable. They less often slice the 9 to 12 year line itself.</p>
<p>Schedule hip radiographs when the dog turns 1, with a repeat by 2 if the first set was taken young, so you have a baseline while exercise choices, and breeding choices if breeding is even on the table, can still move. A listen to the heart belongs in that window and returns with any new cough. Separating meals from hard effort is the home habit that respects bloat risk. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/cane-corso">VCA lifespan framing for the Cane Corso</a> sits in the same talk I have with people counting on about a decade who need the list of what spends it.</p>
<p>A hot afternoon and a full bowl right before a patrol are cuts you can decline. I have watched a wait-and-see afternoon become a surgery that started too late. The stomach allows very little debate. Hips allow more calendar and still hurt. Put the film appointment on paper while the dog is young, at that 1 to 2 year point, and treat a fresh cough as heart news until it is explained.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-cane-corsos-get.html": `<p>Take the withers measurement and let rumor sit down. Male Cane Corsos stand 25 to 27.5 inches. Females stand 23.5 to 26 inches. Those are the registry figures. Recognition by AKC came in 2010, and those height bands are what I use when a contract waves at "extra large." A male measuring 27.5 inches is at the top. A female measuring 23.5 inches is low and can still be correct. Arguments in kitchens are usually about pounds. The printed description talks in inches.</p>
<p>The fill-in lasts. Puppies of this mastiff type keep building from 12 months through 24 months, and much of the chest and skull shows up from 18 months onward to 24. A buyer at 12 months can declare the project finished and still be looking at a narrow front. Around 24 months the outline has usually arrived. Hard braking and repeated jumping wait from age 1 until age 2. The frame is still under construction then. Slick floors plus an endless bowl show up later as complaining hips.</p>
<p>Numbers I have actually written include 88 for a young female who was mostly leg and 120 for a finished male whose chest had dropped. Two animals, two visits. Asked how big the breed gets, I repeat 25 to 27.5 and 23.5 to 26. Paws on a baby are a poor forecast. See the parents on even footing, then follow your own puppy month after month. A photo from above makes every Corso look taller. Stand beside the dog, level the tape, and write the date. I have been fooled by a fluffy adolescent neck more than once, and the second measurement a week later settled the argument. Growth from 12 months to 24 months is depth more than height, so the withers number can look stuck while the dog gets substantially harder to move in a hallway. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/cane-corso/">AKC height standard for the Cane Corso</a> is what I want cited next to the 12, 18, and 24 month marks.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-health-issues.html": `<p>The spine is the worry I put first on a Pembroke Welsh Corgi, because the build is a long back on short legs. Intervertebral disc disease shows up as a yelp, a hunched back, refusal to hop up, wobbly hind legs, or a dog suddenly unable to stand. Treat that as an emergency. Obesity loads the spine, so extra mass is a mechanical load on the discs, and the scale belongs in the medical plan. I hold males to a 30-pound cap and females to 28. A male I worked near 25 pounds took stairs more easily than the heavier coworkers I wish had used a ramp.</p>
<p>Pelvic films catch dysplastic hips, which bring stiffness and a rolling hop people excuse as the breed wiggle. Those films keep the pelvis distinct from the back. Degenerative myelopathy is the slow hind-end slide: scuffed nails, a weave, weakness without the sharp pain of a blown disc. Progressive retinal atrophy steals vision from night toward day until a curb is missed. Von Willebrand disease shows as bleeding that continues, a quick that will not clot, bruises, or a surgery that will not stay dry. Ask for those histories before a surgical suite is where you learn them.</p>
<p>Withers height is 10 to 12 inches, which is why the back looks long on the dog in the hallway. Usual years run 12 to 13. Seeing 15 generally means a light frame and a quiet spine, and that birthday has been the exception in my caseload. I want a neuro-aware exam in year 3, hip images when they can be read properly, and a new spinal history in year 5. DNA testing for degenerative myelopathy, progressive retinal atrophy, and von Willebrand disease belongs with the parents' hip papers.</p>
<p>Ordinary days carry the warnings. A pause at a step, a toenail that drags, a doorframe bumped at dusk, or a nail bleeding longer than last time is the disease list wearing street clothes. Fat is the lever a person can move. Scoop the meal. Make jumps scarce. Install a ramp before a disc makes the choice. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck's coverage of intervertebral disc disease</a> is the writeup I trust when encouragement is the wrong tool for a hunched corgi.</p>
<p>The sudden disc dog and the slow myelopathy dog need different clocks. One needs a neurologist quickly. The other needs rugs, nail care for scuffing feet, and a life that narrows in the rear. Bleeding-disorder results matter before a dental. Retinal results matter before dusk walks turn into collisions. Bring the paperwork you were shown to the first visit so nobody rebuilds a pedigree from chatter.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-temperament.html": `<p>Ankles become equipment when a Pembroke Welsh Corgi shares the house. Herding is the vocation, and it appears as nips at feet and calves meant to shift people, kids, and other animals along a path the dog selected. Near 25 pounds a male nips with the same intent as a male at the 30-pound cap. Females belong at or below 28 pounds and nip from the same idea. Herding explains the teeth. It does nothing to reduce the bruise, so the behavior gets an answer in the week it starts.</p>
<p>The bark is the other watchdog tool. Door, hallway, a dog on a screen, even a passing thought can set it off. A quiet cue goes in during week 3, while a redirect still works, and it gets proofed in month 5 against the bell, the street, and a guest who stays quiet instead of out-shouting the dog. Often the noise is a demand that the herd look up. A finished task beats a lecture: mat, toy search, payment for a closed mouth.</p>
<p>Low legs and a lengthy topline leave the mind full size. These dogs are busy, opinionated, and willing to direct household traffic if the post is vacant. Natural tails are accepted. Docking is historical, so the puppy wagging a whole tail has the right hardware, and the plume helps you read mood. Stiff plume plus a hard eye at heel height means the herd is loading. A soft plume and a sit means you can pay the stop.</p>
<p>That herding urge holds through a common run of 12 to 13 years. One of mine was still organizing ducks at 15, beyond the usual line, and only because the spine had cooperated. At 10 to 12 inches tall the attitude sits where calves are. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pembroke-welsh-corgi/">AKC temperament sketch of the Pembroke Welsh Corgi</a> calls this a herding dog who stays involved. Weeks went well when a legal job existed and quiet had a cue.</p>
<p>Explain the herding to children before they sprint. Sprinting reads as stock on the move. The child learns to freeze with arms folded. The dog learns a freeze earns a toy. Door greetings use the same shape. Scolding the nip while offering zero substitute leaves instinct employed. A low pole, a fenced gather, or a send to a mat moves the teeth off skin.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Your hands cover more ground than the height suggests. I begin a Pembroke Welsh Corgi in the ruff, thickest at the neck, using a rake that finds undercoat and stops shy of scraping. Thigh pants come next, the hiding place for burrs and loose wool. A natural tail, the shape this breed allows, mats at the root and gets its own pass. The dock belongs to older kennel custom. Keeping the plume means more coat, and that is the whole story.</p>
<p>The weekday numbered 3 is pants and chest. The weekday numbered 5 is ruff plus the pocket behind the ears, where a collar felts a skip. Blow weeks hit both zones on both of those days, and fluff still appears later. Guard hairs remain. The rake fills with undercoat. Baths follow the rake, and only for actual dirt. Dry the spine down to skin so a harness is never parked on a damp felt.</p>
<p>Low feet make nail length a gait problem, and hair between pads banks gravel. I flush that pad hair and keep nails quiet on the floor. Once each week I part the hips and look for the hot spot a rainy walk hides under the density. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/pembroke-welsh-corgi">VCA double-coat grooming notes on Pembrokes</a> call the double coat hands-on, which is why the rake lives at the door.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-exercise-guide.html": `<p>The week is herding, resized for a lengthy spine set on low legs. Nipping and barking are how a Pembroke Welsh Corgi moves animals, and a schedule that ignores both ends in sore ankles and a raw voice. Outlets should keep the back level and the mind booked.</p>
<p>Monday is grass, a loose harness, a moderate pace, and sits dropped in so stopping gets practice while heels are right there. Tuesday is a herding ball or a pole kept low inside a fence, several bursts, then a down. The session labeled 3 is the longer thinking day, cones or a fresh field, and steps are walked at a calm pace. Thursday repeats the walk and the quiet cue when another dog goes by. The session labeled 5 is off. No twisting launch after a ball, no stair fitness, no long balance on the rear feet. Saturday may repeat a herding game if the back stayed easy. Sunday is a mosey and a chew.</p>
<p>Extra body mass loads the discs, so an endless bowl cancels the point of the week. Past 30 pounds on a male, or past 28 on a female, the spine is hauling surplus. The one I allowed to creep up showed it leaving the couch. Disc trouble is why the off day exists. Yelp, hunch, or wobble retires the pole until a clinic has looked. Scuffing from degenerative myelopathy turns the week into straight easy walks, and twisting games exit.</p>
<p>Noise falls when herding has a container and rises when a window is the only event. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pembroke-welsh-corgi/">AKC account of Pembroke Welsh Corgi herding drive</a> is the description I fasten the week to, rest included for that spine.</p>
<p>July concrete at noon is a harsh place for a dog whose chest rides near the ground. Brain work moves to morning. Afternoon becomes a chew in a cool room. Puppies inherit the shapes with less time on the clock, because discs and growth plates are both still drafting the adult. I want the adult pattern, labeled rest included, installed before the body is heavy.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Read, then pay. A Pembroke Welsh Corgi folder should hold hip evaluations and results aimed at degenerative myelopathy, von Willebrand disease, and progressive retinal atrophy. Nothing tidy screens a puppy for disc disease, and I talk about it anyway, since a lengthy spine and low legs are already visible in the whelping box. A whole tail is correct. Older kennels docked, and a seller who calls a full tail a defect loses the sale with me.</p>
<p>Hold grown males to 30 pounds. Hold grown females to 28 pounds. Finished withers sit from 10 inches to 12 inches. Hitting 25 pounds by month 12 means the scale is nearly done and the back still needs protection for the rest of the opening year. Launches off furniture, twisty play with bigger dogs, and stair races stay out. A ramp arrives early so the route exists before any disc argues.</p>
<p>Nipping shows early. Swap it for a toy as the puppy reaches month 3, and proof the swap when speed shows up near month 5. Barking gets its quiet cue in that same stretch. People, kids who can freeze, stable dogs, slick floors, and a crate all belong before adult teeth set the face. Meet nobody until then and the house will be managed by noise and by heels.</p>
<p>I sketch the years near 12 to 13, and I treat 15 as an outlier that requires a quiet spine. A heavy body loads the discs, so the puppy scoop is measured immediately. Round bellies are a phase that ends. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">OFA CHIC hip records for Pembroke Welsh Corgi parents</a> should be printed beside the DNA results covering myelopathy, bleeding, and eyes.</p>
<p>Paws, mouth, and a fake exam stand are games while the puppy is light. Admiration without handling produces a nail argument later, when the dog is stronger and harder to lift. Food stays scooped. I want ribs findable under fluff before that fluff turns into the grown double coat. Carry the puppy on long stair flights until the clinic has seen the gait. Littermate wrestling stays on grass. Tile is a bad surface for those crashes.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-shedding-guide.html": `<p>What hits the floor from a Pembroke Welsh Corgi starts in a double coat, harsher guard hairs over dense wool. Wool is the blizzard. It thickens at the ruff, chest, thigh pants, belly, and tail, and a release comes away in tufts two fingers can pull. Guard hairs shed as well, shorter and stiffer, threading into cloth. Judging only the saddle misses the volume, because the saddle looks tight after the rest has already let go.</p>
<p>One female kept near 25 pounds and one male close to the 30-pound cap both buried a floor twice yearly. The lighter dog spared the vacuum nothing that mattered. Wool along a back stretched over a body only 10 to 12 inches at the shoulder is the source. The big releases land in spring plus fall. Indoor heat can add a smaller winter let-go. Between those, a dusting of guard hair and wool wisps shows on black pants.</p>
<p>The same rake pulled a shocking amount during year 12 on one dog and during year 15 on another. Follicles stayed employed. Blow weeks get a carding on the day marked 3 and another on the day marked 5, and fluff still hides behind ears and under a collar in the gaps. Left-in wool mats the ruff. Bathing mid-blow, ahead of the rake, felts wool into guard hair and glues it down the spine.</p>
<p>Lie-down spots receive the donation. Couch, car seat, and bed foot collect pants coat because that is the hair touching fabric. Plume on a natural tail sheds onto whatever the wag strikes. The historical dock removed a source. Keeping the plume keeps both guard hair and wool in play. Extra fat loads discs and also blocks the belly, so wool there mats unseen.</p>
<p>Skin-level raking, a bath, a complete dry, and a second rake actually remove hair. Surface polish leaves the shedding layer parked, so the dog looks neat and the room keeps manufacturing fluff. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/pembroke-welsh-corgi">VCA description of Pembroke Welsh Corgi shedding</a> matches the sacks I close up each spring.</p>
<p>Shake bedding outdoors before the rake, or the wool just removed transfers back when he lies down. Door mats show a gray drift by evening in a blow. A damp glove over upholstery lifts seam leftovers the vacuum misses. None of that substitutes for working the coat. The follicle is the origin. Furniture only receives the delivery. I keep a second rake in the mudroom because the one by the couch disappears into a laundry basket every blow week. Count the tufts if you doubt the volume. A single thigh can fill a palm twice before the guard coat looks any different, and the chest repeats the trick a little later. The low body means your knees are on the floor for this job. Pad them. A rushed standing swipe only polishes the saddle and leaves the pants loaded.</p>`,

  "guides/pembroke-welsh-corgi-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Twelve to thirteen, written 12 to 13, is the span I give for a Pembroke Welsh Corgi so the number stays as plain as the breed usually earns. Arrival at 15 occurs. It has crossed my schedule, and it is rarer after disc injury or after degenerative myelopathy starts taking the rear. An extended topline over brief legs is what those years must carry.</p>
<p>A disc event can compress comfortable life into a day: yelp, hunch, weak legs, sometimes an inability to walk. Extra fat on the spine makes that injury likelier to wreck the middle. Dysplastic hips add a chronic tax that limits action well before it limits the birthday. Progressive retinal atrophy can remove sight and leave the years altered. Von Willebrand disease can turn a dental or a torn nail into a bleed. Myelopathy removes the hind end by degrees and removes the walks with it.</p>
<p>Book the back exam for year 3, and repeat it when year 5 shows up. Keep males to 30 pounds or under that. Keep females to 28 or under that. Withers usually read 10 inches, 12 inches, or somewhere in that band. You hold the scale, and the scale is the longevity tool. Ramps, scooped meals, and true days off complete the plan. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pembroke-welsh-corgi/">AKC year-span notes on Pembrokes</a> belong beside the disc talk, leaving 12 to 13 as the plan and 15 as luck.</p>
<p>A neighbor's ancient dog creates a longer fairy tale, and then year 12 feels like a broken promise. Hearing 12 to 13 at the start, and treating anything past it as a light frame plus fortune, is the kinder script. The neighbor had an anecdote. This dog has discs. Plan around the discs.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-pembroke-welsh-corgis-get.html": `<p>Height is the direct measurement and weight is a ceiling. Pembroke Welsh Corgis stand 10 to 12 inches. Males cap at 30 pounds. Females cap at 28 pounds. A fit male I kept near 25 pounds looked sturdy from bone and coat and still sat under the male ceiling. The low rectangular body shows up at each of those weights. Added mass uses the same spine.</p>
<p>The outline usually settles after month 10 and is honest by month 16. Around month 10 you already see the rectangle inside puppy fluff. By month 16 height has settled inside that 10 to 12 inch band, and the live question is lean adult versus soft adult. I put the puppy on the scale at 3 months and again at 5 months, so a charge toward 25 pounds gets the scoop changed before ceilings of 30 for males and 28 for females arrive by surprise. Round babies remain long-backed babies.</p>
<p>Both sexes share height and split the ceilings. At 28 pounds a female is on her line. At 30 pounds a male is on his. Thick coat grants no free pass. Feel along the topline for ribs. Wool hides a waist. <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/pembroke-welsh-corgi/">AKC size caps written for Pembrokes</a> go on the food bin as 10 to 12 inches of height, 30 and 28 as scale lines, and month 10 to month 16 as the lock-in window.</p>`,
};
